<p>
  Standing in front of my 2nd grade class, ears red, heart pounding, I wanted to dissolve into the floor. My well-intentioned teacher announced to the class that I had a photographic memory and rapid-fired questions at me like I was something to be figured out in public. I knew every answer. I couldn't say a word. It doesn't work like that. We don't work like that. As children we learned to hide. At work we learned to perform a version of ourselves that could pass as neurotypical.
</p>

<p>
  I am one of an estimated 20% of the workforce who is neurodivergent. Most of you had no idea. That's the point.
</p>

<p>
  When the CEO of a $433 billion company says neurodivergent minds are the future, it's worth asking how many of them we're screening out before they ever reach him. According to Understood/Kantar 2025, 29% of Gen Z identify as neurodivergent, along with 12% of Gen X, and 4% of Boomers. The numbers aren't growing, people are just hiding a little bit less.
</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>Some self-reported sources place neurodivergence as high as 53% (Deloitte, 2023).</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
  If this is the future of talent, then we are actively screening out the people we claim to need. Every job posting says accommodations are available upon request. 86% of hiring managers say disclosure has a positive or neutral impact yet 70% of neurodivergent workers believe there is stigma to asking, up 10% from the year before. Among those who did ask, only 56% received accommodations that actually improved their experience. Managers refused one in five requests outright.
</p>

<p>
  The gap between what we say and what we do is the problem. In a moment when AI is eliminating jobs faster than it is creating them, trust is harder to extend when the people asking for it are the same ones redesigning the economy without us.
</p>

<p>
  Old school ADA practices list neurodivergence as a "disability" to be disclosed while stigma keeps anyone from actually raising their hand. The ADA was built for physical accommodation. Disclosure as the price of access made sense for a wheelchair ramp. It was never designed for the way a brain works under pressure.
</p>

<p>
  You cannot call my thinking a disability and a competitive advantage in the same breath.
</p>

<p>
  The well-intentioned answer has been to build a separate door: extended timelines, interview coaches, preparation programs designed to help neurodivergent candidates pass the same process everyone else takes. But that assumes the process is sound and the candidate is the problem. And it still requires a label to walk through.
</p>

<p>
  Palantir's Neurodivergent Fellowship is the latest version of this answer, explicitly framed not as a diversity initiative but as a competitive advantage. It's a better door. Still a separate one. If neurodivergent minds are truly the future, why do they need a different entrance to get there?
</p>

<p>
  Decades of research in I/O psychology tell us exactly what predicts job performance yet we ignore it in favor of the least reliable predictor: the job interview stage performance. When interviewer impression of a single event drives the hiring decision, bias is baked in.
</p>

<p>
  Signal separation untangles interview performance from job performance. A scoring engine that measures objective behavioral signals — responsiveness, follow through, preparation, consistency — produces the same result regardless of who is scoring or who is being scored. It doesn't treat neurodivergent candidates as something to be accommodated. It just stops penalizing them for not performing neurotypically under pressure. We don't work like that.
</p>

<p>
  Instead of creating a different process for different people, we create a better process for everyone — one that doesn't require a label to access.
</p>

<p>
  The goal was never a better door. It was never needing one.
</p>

<p>
  The interview has always been the wrong test. We finally have the tools to build the right one.
</p>
